**Where static and global variables live.** In the process's **data segment**, not the stack or heap. Initialised globals and statics go in the **.data** section; uninitialised (or zero-initialised) ones go in **.bss**, which the loader zeroes. String literals typically sit in read-only data. They exist for the entire program lifetime. Contrast: local variables live on the **stack**, and `new`/`malloc` memory lives on the **heap**. Code is in the **text** segment.

**Raw versus smart pointers.**
- A raw pointer is just an address. It carries no ownership information, does not free memory, and can dangle or be null. Leaks and double frees are on you.
- Smart pointers wrap a pointer and apply **RAII**: the destructor releases the resource.
- `std::unique_ptr<T>`: sole ownership, move-only, zero overhead. Default choice.
- `std::shared_ptr<T>`: reference-counted shared ownership; freed when the last owner goes away. Beware cycles.
- `std::weak_ptr<T>`: non-owning observer that breaks `shared_ptr` cycles.

**Leak and fix.**
```cpp
#include <memory>
#include <stdexcept>
struct Widget { /* ... */ };

void leaky(bool fail) {
    Widget* w = new Widget();
    if (fail) throw std::runtime_error("oops"); // early exit: delete never runs
    delete w;
}

void safe(bool fail) {
    auto w = std::make_unique<Widget>();
    if (fail) throw std::runtime_error("oops"); // destructor runs during unwinding
}
```
In `leaky`, an exception (or an early `return`) skips `delete`, so the object leaks every time. In `safe`, `unique_ptr` is destroyed when the scope ends, however it exits, so nothing leaks. You can confirm with Valgrind or AddressSanitizer.

**Rules of thumb.** Prefer stack objects and `unique_ptr`, use `make_unique`/`make_shared`, avoid owning raw pointers, and use raw pointers or references only for non-owning access.
